## Visual Design for Country Borders

```
US States:   stroke #1E3A5F (dark navy blue),  stroke-width 0.8px  → subtle blue border feel
Canada Prov: stroke #6B2121 (dark crimson red), stroke-width 0.8px → subtle red border feel

Country outline (thicker, exterior only via CSS):
  [id^="US-"] → box-shadow or drop-shadow effect using atlas-accent tint
  [id^="CA-"] → distinct crimson tint
```

The borders are intentionally **thin and subtle** — not loud. The goal is that when the map transitions from individual state exploration to "complete map", you can still clearly tell which country each region belongs to.

---

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Map initial fit** — Currently the SVG loads with `min-w-[1200px]` which makes the full map wider than the viewport, requiring horizontal scroll. Would you prefer:
> - **(A) Full-fit (recommended)**: SVG scales to fill the container width, showing all of North America at once, and zoom-in actually zooms in.
> - **(B) Keep large + scrollable**: Keep current behaviour but fix the top clipping.

> [!NOTE]
> **Canada data** — The current `CLUSTER_CHECKPOINTS` only covers US regions. Should Canadian provinces also get cluster groupings (e.g. "Western Canada", "Central Canada", "Maritime Provinces")? This is separate work and not included in this plan.

---

## Verification Plan

### Automated
- `npm run build` in `app/` to confirm no TypeScript errors

### Manual
1. Load map explorer → confirm **no flicker** when clicking states
2. Click multiple states in rapid succession → confirm **no pop animation** on already-explored states
3. Zoom in/out with `+`/`-` buttons → confirm zoom **actually increases/decreases** map scale visibly
4. Scroll to the north of the map → confirm **Yukon, Northwest Territories, Nunavut are visible**
5. Hover over a US state vs a Canadian province → confirm **different coloured borders**
